[Music]  the latest coronavirus surge is filling  many hospitals to or near  capacity and pushing some health care  workers to a breaking point this morning  carter evan shares the stories of two  nurses who are coping  by pouring their personal experiences  into poetry and song  at loma linda university health in  southern california  it's another day grappling with the  virus ted warku is an er trauma nurse  every shift i walk into the hospital  you're going to be confronted by  something  that you're not fully prepared for  you're never fully prepared for somebody  that will lose their life in front of  you  on your next shift and they impact you  i've seen hope  or from the depth of despair i've seen  peace coming out from nowhere  to cope with the emotional toll tad  picks up his guitar  medicine inspiring his music but i can  see  [Music]  almost 2 000 miles away near minneapolis  hospice nurse aaron pomerantz juggles as  many as 19  patients holding their hands as they  died because  covid prevents families from visiting  what's it like being the person  who holds the iphone for that final zoom  i'm there with my mask and my shield on  and i'm holding up the phone  to see their grandma and their mom one  last time  this is not how we say goodbye to to our  loved ones  she processes her experiences through  poetry  over the phone through the camera lens  one last time you see her brown  loving eyes that once filled the room  with sparkle and joy  the hospital where she works recently  her body is ready you are not  it's not fair you say through tears  i agree my tears and breath clouding the  goggles  back in california  [Music]  began performing for the hospital staff  a support group formed to help medical  workers struggling with the pandemic  his music struck a chord with co-workers  when i don't understand why the world is  acting the way it is and when you hear  dad and you hear his music  it just makes you feel like it's gonna  be okay tomorrow's gonna 